---
source: src/cli/tests.rs
expression: help
---
Populate the real HuggingFace hub cache (~/.cache/huggingface/hub) so vLLM/transformers/`hf download` find a revision-pinned snapshot with zero network calls

Usage: sync [OPTIONS] <MODEL_ID> [FILE]...

Arguments:
  <MODEL_ID>
          Model ID, e.g. "Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct"

  [FILE]...
          Exact repo-relative file paths (repeatable positional)

Options:
      --revision <REV>
          Git revision to sync: branch, tag, or 40-hex commit SHA [default: main]

      --for <PRESET>
          Fetch only the files a target runtime reads (preset selection)

      --include <GLOB>
          Include only files matching GLOB (repeatable, fnmatch semantics: `*` crosses `/`)

      --exclude <GLOB>
          Exclude files matching GLOB (repeatable; applies after every selection mode)

      --cache-dir <DIR>
          HuggingFace hub cache directory [default: $HF_HUB_CACHE, then $HF_HOME/hub, then the platform default]

      --no-symlinks
          Copy files into snapshots/ instead of symlinking to blobs/

      --force
          Re-download even if the blob already exists in the cache

      --dry-run
          List what would be fetched/skipped; no writes

      --token <TOKEN>
          HuggingFace token [default: $HF_TOKEN, then config]

      --no-verify
          Skip SHA256 verification

      --json
          JSON Lines events on stdout (progress included, throttled)

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress progress output; errors and the final summary only

      --progress <MODE>
          Progress output mode: auto (tty rewrites), plain (one line every ~10 s, works without a tty), none

          Possible values:
          - auto:  Single-line `\r` rewrites on a tty; nothing when piped (default)
          - plain: One newline progress line every ~10 s, tty-independent (for docker run / CI logs); adds a verification heartbeat during the post-download SHA256 drain
          - none:  No progress output at all
          
          [default: auto]

      --rate-limit
          Enable download rate limiting (uses --rate-limit-mbps or the config-file value)

      --no-rate-limit
          Disable download rate limiting (overrides the config file)

      --rate-limit-mbps <MBPS>
          Download rate limit in Mbps (implies --rate-limit)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
